We at the M plus 1, M plus first round, we have some parameters. That’s the log likelihood

210
00:24:22,059 --> 00:24:29,529
of those parameters. And we know that Q function is lower bound. We proved it for any q, any

211
00:24:29,529 --> 00:24:37,909
choice of the distribution, a small q. And then this Q value was chosen by the pervious

212
00:24:37,909 --> 00:24:44,679
iterations M step. So this equality follows. This is the maximum value of Q which maximizes

213
00:24:44,679 --> 00:24:51,160
the, maximum over all parameters curly theta. And then this by definition is greater than

214
00:24:51,160 --> 00:24:59,490
any Q here, and because this E step bond is tight, we get that this is equal to the logarithm,

215
00:24:59,490 --> 00:25:07,419
equal to the likelihood in the previous step, which is just the likelihood of the previous

216
00:25:07,419 --> 00:25:11,919
step.

217
00:25:11,919 --> 00:25:16,429
Okay.

218
00:25:16,429 --> 00:25:26,960
So any questions? Now is this clear, why it is increasing the likelihood at each point,

219
00:25:26,960 --> 00:25:37,669
at each iteration. Alright. So now, so that covers the basics of EM. Now let us look at

220
00:25:37,669 --> 00:25:43,659
some strange cases.

221
00:25:43,659 --> 00:25:51,429
Sometimes what happens is when you are running EM, you tend to get very strange solutions

222
00:25:51,429 --> 00:25:57,340
and this could be one of the reasons. So I am going to motivate this mathematically.

223
00:25:57,340 --> 00:26:06,490
So suppose you take your log likelihood for the, that you want to maximize and you set,

224
00:26:06,490 --> 00:26:10,929
now suppose you have two components, okay. It does not matter.

225
00:26:10,929 --> 00:26:16,280
So take one of the components and set mu 1 the mean to be equal to x1, one of the data

226
00:26:16,280 --> 00:26:26,509
points, and set Sigma 1 to be equal to some diagonal matrix of dimensionality p, and take

227
00:26:26,509 --> 00:26:32,690
some pi 1. So this can be just split into two parts. We are looking at just one Gaussian

228
00:26:32,690 --> 00:26:40,799
here, and when you plug in these values you essentially get this expression. Now what

229
00:26:40,799 --> 00:26:48,999
happens if sigma 1 square, the variance tends to 0? This likelihood essentially tends to

230
00:26:48,999 --> 00:26:56,390
infinity, I mean this total likelihood, because this value goes to infinity, right.

231
00:26:56,390 --> 00:27:04,490
So this is a problem in general with maximum likelihood solutions. Your, the likelihood

232
00:27:04,490 --> 00:27:09,450
will tend to infinity although the fit is really bad.

233
00:27:09,450 --> 00:27:14,090
This looks like… So the pictorial representation is something like this. What you are doing

234
00:27:14,090 --> 00:27:20,149
is you are taking two Gaussians and you are fitting just one data point with one Gaussian,

235
00:27:20,149 --> 00:27:29,340
and the other Gaussian is fitting the rest of the data points. So this is, in most real

236
00:27:29,340 --> 00:27:35,760
life cases this is not a good thing to do, because yeah, it’s very unlikely that the

237
00:27:35,760 --> 00:27:40,240
data has been generated by two Gaussians like this, one data point from one Gaussian and

238
00:27:40,240 --> 00:27:44,240
the rest from the other Gaussian, right.

239
00:27:44,240 --> 00:27:49,679
So any… So when you try to do this with just a single Gaussian, do you think you will

240
00:27:49,679 --> 00:27:57,019
get this problem? Why?

241
00:27:57,019 --> 00:28:10,610
Yeah, but suppose you have, suppose I give you, I take uni-dimensional case, and I fit

242
00:28:10,610 --> 00:28:18,749
this one Gaussian here. This, it is, there will be a nonzero probability of a point coming

243
00:28:18,749 --> 00:28:39,840
from somewhere here, right. You know this is the mean. So intuitively we would think

244
00:28:39,840 --> 00:28:46,249
that the blue Gaussian is what might have generated this data with so much variance,

245
00:28:46,249 --> 00:28:47,970
right.

246
00:28:47,970 --> 00:28:56,770
But there is a nonzero probability that the data has been generated from such a Gaussian.

247
00:28:56,770 --> 00:29:02,140
So why will we not have this problem there?

248
00:29:02,140 --> 00:29:09,710
Yeah, so the maximum likelihood solution will never give you this. Maximum likelihood solution

249
00:29:09,710 --> 00:29:16,390
is most likely to give you something like this, right. When you work out the likelihood,

250
00:29:16,390 --> 00:29:21,559
the likelihood for the pink Gaussian is definitely going to be lesser than the likelihood for

251
00:29:21,559 --> 00:29:22,779
this, right.

252
00:29:22,779 --> 00:29:29,951
And again this is just due to the mathematical form of the Gaussian mixture. So this, because

253
00:29:29,951 --> 00:29:34,769
of the summation this is really happening, because it is possible that you can fit the

254
00:29:34,769 --> 00:29:41,330
data like that in a way that the likelihood goes to infinity.

255
00:29:41,330 --> 00:29:49,630
So how do you deal with this? The simplest way in a frequentist framework is to just

256
00:29:49,630 --> 00:29:55,450
keep, when you are running EM, you check whether it is happening or not and if you, if it is

257
00:29:55,450 --> 00:30:00,179
happening then you just reinitialize the parameters, you keep trying to detect such collapsing

258
00:30:00,179 --> 00:30:06,710
components and try to do it. And in general actually it is better to restart EM several

259
00:30:06,710 --> 00:30:14,070
times, because EM is, as you know it can get stuck at a fixed point or a saddle point.

260
00:30:14,070 --> 00:30:19,460
So with different initialization parameters you can get much better solutions as we saw

261
00:30:19,460 --> 00:30:22,029
in this simulation as well.

262
00:30:22,029 --> 00:30:27,600
The bayesian solution is to take priors, right. You take priors on each of the parameters

263
00:30:27,600 --> 00:30:32,230
and it turns out you can work out the math and see, that the expectation, the E step

264
00:30:32,230 --> 00:30:40,039
remains the same and the only difference necessary is the additional term in the M step that

265
00:30:40,039 --> 00:30:51,710
we need to maximize, and this usually solves the problem by choosing right priors.

266
00:30:51,710 --> 00:30:56,909
So now it’s come to finding K. Till now we have assumed that we know the number of

267
00:30:56,909 --> 00:31:00,690
components. And how do we find K?

268
00:31:00,690 --> 00:31:08,830
This is, there is no really good solution to finding K, and what statisticians usually

269
00:31:08,830 --> 00:31:14,009
prefer and what works well in practice is to generate many candidate models. You look

270
00:31:14,009 --> 00:31:19,070
at the data and you assume that okay, there can’t be lesser than 3 components here,

271
00:31:19,070 --> 00:31:24,269
there can’t be more than 12 components here. So let us run EM for all these different values

272
00:31:24,269 --> 00:31:35,470
of K and you choose that K which minimizes some criterion, okay. And this, there are

273
00:31:35,470 --> 00:31:41,419
different criteria that people have discussed. For example, it is something like the regularization

274
00:31:41,419 --> 00:31:44,960
that you do in another models. You basically penalize high values of K.

275
00:31:44,960 --> 00:31:50,940
So the AIC, Akaike information criterion is this, this is just the log likelihood plus

276
00:31:50,940 --> 00:31:56,659
k. So minimizing this will give you the least number of components which can explain the

277
00:31:56,659 --> 00:32:07,249
data well, okay. There is a bayesian information criterion which uses k logn similar general

278
00:32:07,249 --> 00:32:13,669
idea. And then there are other approaches for finding K which are bayesian nonparametric

279
00:32:13,669 --> 00:32:21,929
approaches where you assume some Dirichlet process priors and then the method itself

280
00:32:21,929 --> 00:32:31,450
automatically estimates K, right.

281
00:32:31,450 --> 00:32:38,009
So the algorithm that we discussed in that form was given in 1977. So you can imagine

282
00:32:38,009 --> 00:32:41,929
that a lot of work has been done on EM since 1977.

283
00:32:41,929 --> 00:32:48,359
There are lot of different kinds of EM algorithms. There are online versions that work on large

284
00:32:48,359 --> 00:32:56,029
streaming data sets. Like I said EM is designed to find local maximum. So there are annealed

285
00:32:56,029 --> 00:33:01,239
versions that increases the chances of finding global maximum. The simplest solution is random

286
00:33:01,239 --> 00:33:08,120
restarts but annealing does something more. Variation, so sometimes, so in the case of

287
00:33:08,120 --> 00:33:12,870
Gaussian we saw that the E step and the M steps, they were computationally tractable,

288
00:33:12,870 --> 00:33:16,509
we could derive analytical formulas for these.

289
00:33:16,509 --> 00:33:22,009
But in a lot of cases… If there is time I can show one. We will see that they are

290
00:33:22,009 --> 00:33:26,470
not computationally tractable and sometimes you need to do additional things. So there

291
00:33:26,470 --> 00:33:31,470
are variational versions of EM, there are stochastic versions of EM, Monte Carlo version

292
00:33:31,470 --> 00:33:36,429
where you have intractable E steps. There is something call generalized EM which was

293
00:33:36,429 --> 00:33:43,309
one of the earliest algorithms where you have computationally intractable M steps.

294
00:33:43,309 --> 00:33:48,149
Then when we have sequential parameters, dependent parameters, then there are other versions

295
00:33:48,149 --> 00:33:57,929
of EM. And in general EM is quite slow, right. So your, each step within EM, within the iteration

296
00:33:57,929 --> 00:34:03,539
is computationally not very expensive, but convergence is usually very slow. And it’s

297
00:34:03,539 --> 00:34:09,040
especially slow when you have lots of missing data or lots of latent variables to infer,

298
00:34:09,040 --> 00:34:18,450
okay. So there are many approaches to deal with it - these Aitken acceleration techniques

299
00:34:18,450 --> 00:34:25,970
over relaxed EM and so on. So to summarize, like what I said.

300
00:34:25,970 --> 00:34:31,440
The major advantage of EM is that it monotonically increases the likelihood, it guarantees that.

301
00:34:31,440 --> 00:34:39,240
If you take any distribution, any mixture model or anything, any likelihood computation

302
00:34:39,240 --> 00:34:44,110
where there are hidden variables or latent variables and you apply EM and if you follow

303
00:34:44,110 --> 00:34:49,380
the formulas carefully you will guarantee that the likelihood is increased except at

304
00:34:49,380 --> 00:34:50,630
fixed points.

305
00:34:50,630 --> 00:34:55,820
And it is usually numerically very stable compared to other techniques like gradient

306
00:34:55,820 --> 00:35:00,890
descent. It is easily implemented, and the interesting thing is that many problems can

307
00:35:00,890 --> 00:35:04,290
be modeled as incomplete data problems. We saw that in the case of Gaussian mixture,

308
00:35:04,290 --> 00:35:11,340
there is no missing data in the beginning but we assume the latent variables to be missing.

309
00:35:11,340 --> 00:35:16,690
The disadvantages as I mentioned is slow convergence and there is no guarantees of finding global

310
00:35:16,690 --> 00:35:25,590
maximum, and the steps may be analytically intractable, okay.

311
00:35:25,590 --> 00:35:35,110
Yeah, so the two standard references have very nice explanation for EM, and there are

312
00:35:35,110 --> 00:35:39,620
very nice tutorials also available. Matrix Cookbook you should be familiar with to get

313
00:35:39,620 --> 00:35:44,430
all your matrix derivatives, and this is these standard reference if you want to go really

314
00:35:44,430 --> 00:35:58,010
deep into EM - McLachlan and Krishnan’s book on EM, the whole book is on EM algorithm.

315
00:35:58,010 --> 00:36:02,160
The EM can always solve it but it not maybe able to solve well. That’s the… There

316
00:36:02,160 --> 00:36:18,320
is lots of missing data then usually the, it does not give good results.

317
00:36:18,320 --> 00:36:26,610
Right, so there is some, there is lot of work on, so these estimates that you are getting

318
00:36:26,610 --> 00:36:30,810
you may need to, you may sometimes want to know how good those estimates are. Right,

319
00:36:30,810 --> 00:36:35,880
so you want to get these standard errors on those estimates. So there is, so in fact that

320
00:36:35,880 --> 00:36:40,320
is one of the flaws of EM. It does not automatically give you that but there are methods to deal

321
00:36:40,320 --> 00:36:45,380
with that. For example, there are some bootstrap methods that can give you estimates of the

